diff --git a/docs/UPGRADE.md b/docs/UPGRADE.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..311dc78
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/UPGRADE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,65 @@
+# Upgrade record
+
+## Rollout invariants
+
+- Preserve the exact production `secret_key_base` across deployments and rollbacks. Do not regenerate it during either upgrade.
+- Deploy one runtime/framework change at a time. This release is Ruby 3.2 with Rails 6.1; the following release is Rails 7.0.
+- Keep a shared cache and the same production database. A cache miss is acceptable, but unreadable cache entries are not.
+
+## Current release: Ruby 3.2 / Rails 6.1
+
+The primary bundle remains Rails 6.1.7.10 and now requires Ruby 3.2. `.ruby-version` is 3.2.9.
+
+This release deliberately keeps Rails 6.1's primary cookie and message formats:
+
+- SHA-1 key generation.
+- HMAC/AES-CBC encrypted cookies with Marshal serialization.
+
+That means output from this release remains readable by the immediately preceding Rails 6.1 release on Ruby 3.0, so rolling this Ruby-only release back does not invalidate existing sessions or mailed links.
+
+`Gemfile.next` uses Rails 7.0.10 and can be run with:
+
+```sh
+BUNDLE_GEMFILE=Gemfile.next bundle exec rails test
+```
+
+Both lockfiles resolve on Ruby 3.2. CI exercises Rails 6.1 and Rails 7.0 against every supported database.
+
+## Rails 7.0 rollback bridge
+
+Rails 7.0 loads its 7.0 framework defaults. The application therefore uses the Rails 7 primary formats: SHA-256 key generation, authenticated AES-GCM cookies, and JSON cookie serialization. The compatibility initializer prepares bidirectional readers:
+
+- Rails 6.1 can read Rails 7's SHA-256/AES-GCM cookies.
+- Rails 7.0 can read Rails 6.1's SHA-1/AES-CBC cookies.
+- Cookie purpose metadata is accepted in either form.
+- The `mail_links` verifier accepts tokens produced with either framework's key derivation. Mail-link tokens are valid for 30 days.
+
+As a result, Rails 7.0 may be rolled back to this Rails 6.1 release without signing users out or invalidating links in mail already sent. The production `secret_key_base` must remain identical for these rotations to work.
+
+The Rails 7.0 transition also makes legacy session hashes JSON-safe. Session data is normalized with indifferent access when read, and the app's legacy `belongs_to` associations are explicitly optional where that was the Rails 6 behavior. Rails 7's required-by-default setting remains enabled for new associations.
+
+### Temporary Rails 7.0 framework-default overrides
+
+Rails 7.0 has two shared-state overrides while Rails 6.1 remains a rollback target:
+
+- `config.active_support.cache_format_version = 6.1`: Rails 6.1 cannot decode Rails 7.0 cache entries. Both frameworks must continue to write the 6.1 format while rollback is possible.
+- `config.action_dispatch.cookies_serializer = :hybrid`: Rails 7 must still accept Marshal cookies issued by this release and can transparently rewrite them as JSON. New Rails 7 cookies are JSON and remain readable by the Rails 6.1 Marshal fallback serializer.
+
+These are intentional rollback guards, not permanent target defaults.
+
+## Next Rails release
+
+For the Rails 7.0 production release:
+
+1. Promote the Rails 7.0 dependency and lockfile to the primary `Gemfile` without changing the compatibility behavior in this release.
+2. Keep the 6.1 cache format, hybrid cookie serializer, cookie rotations, and mail-token rotation through the full rollback period.
+3. Verify the release candidate and rollback with the same `secret_key_base` and an account session plus a mailed unwatch link from before the deployment.
+
+After Rails 6.1 is no longer an allowed rollback target and 30 days have elapsed since the last Rails 6.1 mail was sent, a later release may:
+
+- adopt Rails 7.0's `active_support.cache_format_version` and `:json` cookie serializer;
+- remove the Rails 6.1 cookie and message rotations;
+- repurpose `Gemfile.next` for the following framework release;
+- address Rails 7.0 deprecations that are not required for this release.
+
+Do not remove the rotations or shared-state overrides as part of the initial Rails 7.0 deployment.
